    <article class="portfolioItem">
        <header class="portfolioItem__header">
            <h3 class="portfolioItem__title">The Look and Song of Language</h3>
            <div class="portfolioItem__description">
                <h4>Keywords:</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>bigrams</li>
                    <li>trigrams</li>
                    <li>computational linguistics</li>
                    <li>phonotactics</li>
                    <li>English</li>
                    <li>French</li>
                    <li>Spanish</li>
                    <li>Scots</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </header>
        <div class="portfolioItem__description">
            <h4>In a nutshell</h4>
            <p>How do you we recognize our languages? Throw away any knowledge of grammar and syntax, it boils down to sounds; we recognize a language by the sounds it produces. And we can find an equivalent way to identify a language in the written form — we just have to analyze the bigrams, trigrams, and sometimes their placements. </p>

            <p>Using the Methodius Library and Methodius CLI, I sought to answer these questions</p>
            <h4>Learning More:</h4>
            <p>I wrote <a href="https://blog.frankmtaylor.com/2025/02/24/the-look-and-song-of-language/"> a blog post </a> that documents the results of my analysis, including a survey I conducted where I asked folks to construct new words according to the dominant bigrams of English. </p>
            <h4>Publication</h4>
            <p>Not submitted to any journals.</p>
        </div>
