    <article class="portfolioItem">
        <header class="portfolioItem__header">
            <h3 class="portfolioItem__title">Bro, Don't Call Me Bruh: Exploring preferred Bronouns</h3>
            <div class="portfolioItem__description">
                <h4>Keywords:</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>sociolinguistics</li>
                    <li>Social Media</li>
                    <li>Bluesky</li>
                    <li>bro</li>
                    <li>bruh</li>
                    <li>pronouns</li>
                    <li>vocatives</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </header>
        <div class="portfolioItem__description">
            <h4>In a nutshell</h4>
            <p>What is the difference between <i>bro</i> and <i>bruh</i>? Despite their shared etymology, how are they different? Do they communicate different feelings? Are they used in different contexts? </p>
            <p>I captured close to 150,000 posts from the social media network Bluesky which contain <i>dude</i>, <i>bro</i>, <i>bruh</i>, <i>chat</i>, <i>sis</i>, and <i>fam</i>. I conducted multiple types of feature analysis to determine that <i>bro</i> and <i>bruh</i> are in fact used very differently.</p>
            <h4>Publication</h4>
            <p>Presented at the International Conference of Language and Social Psychology in May of 2026</p>
            <p>Paper submitted to the Journal of Language and Social Psychology in June of 2026. Awaiting publication.</p>
        </div>
